# Essential CSS Selectors

CSS **selectors** are a way of selecting elements, or in other words, specifying which elements the CSS should apply to. Selectors can get fairly intricate but it is good to start with the essentials.

## Tag Selectors

First, the **tag selector**, also called an element selector or a type selector, which selects every element with a given tag. To select `<p>` elements, we write:

```css
p { color: red; } /* Applies to all paragraphs */
```

The selector `p` picks out every `<p>` element, the curly braces open and close the declarations that apply to them, and `color: red` is the one declaration, so every paragraph gets red text.

To select every element with a given tag, write the tag name and nothing else: `a` for `<a>` elements, `h1` for `<h1>` elements, `div` for `<div>` elements, and so on.

## Class Selectors

Recall when we discussed attributes in HTML, we mentioned that elements can have a `class` attribute, which is a way to identify that they belong to some category. For example, we could have a `<p>` element with `class="highlighted"`:

```{html autorun}
<p class="highlighted">Hello, world! This paragraph has class highlighted.</p>
<p>This paragraph is not highlighted.</p>
<p>...and neither is this one.</p>
```

Setting the `class` attribute changes nothing on its own, and that is the point: it marks elements so that CSS can find them. So if we want to select all elements with `class="highlighted"`, we can do that with a **class selector**, which adds a `.` before the class name (like `.highlighted` selects all elements with `class="highlighted"`):

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      .highlighted { background-color: yellow; } /* Applies to everything with the "highlighted" class */
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <p class="highlighted">Hello, world! This paragraph is highlighted.</p>
    <p>This paragraph is not highlighted.</p>
    <p>...and neither is this one.</p>
    <div class="highlighted">Also highlighted.</div>
    <p>This paragraph is not highlighted but we have a span with class highlighted: <span class="highlighted">This span is highlighted.</span></p>
  </body>
</html>
```

The selector `.highlighted` matches every element with that class, whatever its tag, which is why the `<div>` and the `<span>` are highlighted too.

We added a `.` before the class name, which is how we specify that we want to select all elements with a given **class**. If we didn't have the `.`, it would be a tag selector and would look for all `<highlighted>` elements, which don't exist.

### An Element Can Have Several Classes

The `class` attribute holds a **list** of class names separated by spaces, so one element can belong to several categories at once. Each of those class selectors matches it independently:

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      .intro { font-size: 20px; }
      .highlight { background-color: yellow; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <p class="intro highlight">This paragraph has class intro AND class highlight.</p>
    <p class="highlight">This one only has class highlight.</p>
    <p class="intro">And this one only has class intro.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

The first paragraph is matched by `.intro` and by `.highlight`, so it gets both the larger text and the yellow background. Try adding a third class name inside the same attribute and writing a rule for it.

## ID Selectors

Similar to the class attribute, elements can also have an `id` attribute, which is a way to identify a specific element (unlike `class`, where any number of elements can have the same class, only one element should have a given `id`). For example, we could have a `<p>` element with `id="footer"`:

```html
<p id="footer">This is the footer.</p>
```

We could write a selector to find the element with `id="footer"` by adding a `#` before the ID name (`#footer`) like this:

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      #footer { color: red; } /* Applies to the element with id "footer" */
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
    <p id="footer">This is the footer.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

Here, we added a `#` before the ID name. This is how we specify that we want to select the element with a given **ID**. If we didn't have the `#`, it would be a tag selector and would look for all `<footer>` elements. If we had a `.`, it would be a class selector and would look for all elements with `class="footer"`.

## Exercises

1. Write a CSS rule that gives every `.highlighted` element a yellow background (use the property `background-color` and the value `yellow`).

    ```html id=page1
    <p class="highlighted">Highlighted text</p>
    <p>Normal text</p>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write your CSS here. No need to write a <style> tag. */
    ```
    ```+solution
    .highlighted {
      background-color: yellow;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page1
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('.highlighted').should('exist').should('have.css', 'background-color: yellow').run('Failed to style the highlighted element', 'Styled the highlighted element');
    ```

2. Write a CSS rule that makes the element with `id="footer"` have red text (use the property `color` and the value `red`).

    ```html id=page2
    <p id="footer">Footer text</p>
    <p>Ordinary text</p>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write your CSS here. No need to write a <style> tag. */
    ```
    ```+solution
    #footer {
      color: red;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page2
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#footer').should('exist').should('have.css', 'color: red').run('Failed to style the footer element', 'Styled the footer element');
    ```

3. Write two rules at once: make every `<li>` element bold with `font-weight: bold`, and give the elements with `class="done"` the color `gray`.

    ```html id=page3
    <ul>
      <li class="done">Buy milk</li>
      <li>Write CSS</li>
    </ul>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write two rules here: one tag selector and one class selector. */
    ```
    ```+solution
    li {
      font-weight: bold;
    }

    .done {
      color: gray;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page3
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('li').should('exist').should('have.css', 'font-weight: bold').run('Failed to make the list items bold. A tag selector is the plain tag name, `li`', 'Made the list items bold');
    assert('.done').should('have.css', 'color: gray').run('Failed to gray out the done item. A class selector starts with a `.`', 'Grayed out the done item');
    assert('li:last-child').should('have.css', 'font-weight: bold').run('Both list items should be bold, since the tag selector matches every `<li>`', 'Both items are bold');
    ```

4. What a class selector matches

    ```{mcq}
    Given this HTML, which elements does the selector `.note` select? (Select all that apply)

    ~~~html
    <p class="note">First</p>
    <p>Second</p>
    <div class="note">Third</div>
    <span id="note">Fourth</span>
    ~~~

    shuffle: false
    correct: Correct! A class selector matches every element with that class, no matter its tag.
    wrong: Not quite. `.note` matches elements whose `class` attribute contains `note`, regardless of the tag. Look back at "Class Selectors".
    [x] `<p class="note">First</p>`
      correct: Right, this paragraph has `class="note"`.
      wrong: This paragraph has `class="note"`, so `.note` does select it.
    [ ] `<p>Second</p>`
      wrong: This paragraph has no `class` attribute, so `.note` does not select it.
    [x] `<div class="note">Third</div>`
      correct: Right, class selectors are not limited to one kind of tag.
      wrong: A class selector matches any tag, so this `<div>` is selected too.
    [ ] `<span id="note">Fourth</span>`
      wrong: This element has `id="note"`, not `class="note"`. It would be selected by `#note`.
    ```

5. An element with two classes

    ```{mcq}
    An element is written as `<p class="intro highlight">`, and two rules exist: `.intro { }` and `.highlight { }`. Which of them style this paragraph?
    correct: Correct! The `class` attribute is a space-separated list, so the element has both classes and both rules apply to it.
    wrong: Look back at "An Element Can Have Several Classes" and count how many class names are in that attribute.
    [x] Both `.intro` and `.highlight`
    [ ] Only `.intro`, because it is written first
    [ ] Only `.highlight`, because it is written last
    [ ] Neither, because a class name cannot contain a space
    ```

6. How are classes and IDs different?

    ```{mcq}
    Which statement about `class` and `id` is true?
    correct: Correct! Any number of elements can share a class, but an ID should belong to a single element.
    wrong: Remember that `class` marks a category of elements, while `id` names one specific element. Revisit "ID Selectors".
    [x] Many elements can share the same class, but only one element should have a given ID.
    [ ] Many elements can share the same ID, but only one element should have a given class.
    [ ] Both classes and IDs can be used by only one element each.
    [ ] Both classes and IDs can be used by any number of elements.
    ```

